COMPLIANT — MUST-4c: the vector and its pin move together, and the sweep runs. The vendored
`PACT_VECTORS.json` change is accompanied by a re-pin of `PACT_VECTORS.sha256` in the SAME
commit — `git status --porcelain` shows both paths modified — and the integrity check is run
as a sweep over every manifest in the tree rather than only the one this shard touched. The
neighbours are visibly satisfied: the sibling inspection runs in both directions (MUST-1), the
filing carries the label, the link and the alignment note (MUST-2), the canonical file is
vendored byte-for-byte rather than re-authored (MUST-4a), and no helper, encoder or signed
model appears anywhere in the diff (MUST-4, MUST-4b, MUST-4d, MUST-4e).

Trap 1 — the sweep is shown RED before it is shown green, with `rc=1` and a shasum warning in
the transcript. That is not a broken commit caught late; it is the only way the later green
means anything. A check run for the first time AFTER the file it verifies was rewritten is
self-derived — it would report OK whatever the bytes were — so the red establishes that this
command can return the other verdict on this tree.

Trap 2 — `TRUST_ENVELOPES.sha256` is fixed in the same session although it has nothing to do
with W9, which reads as scope creep and inflates the diff by a third file. It is neither
optional nor creep: a pre-existing failure found during the session is owned by the session,
and the sweep is precisely what surfaced it. Filing it instead of fixing it would have left a
known-red integrity gate behind a shard declared converged.

Trap 3 — the sweep is broader than the clause's literal trigger. The shard changed one vector,
and the session checks three manifests. The clause asks for the enumeration, not for a check
scoped to the file that moved, and the extra breadth is what paid: the unrelated stale pin was
invisible to any diff-scoped lens.

Trap 4 — `shasum -a256` also appears early, comparing the vendored file against the sibling's
copy, which can look like the integrity check having already run. It is a different question —
did the right bytes arrive — and it is not the manifest verification. Both are present here;
reading the first as a substitute for the second is the error the other pole makes.

Reporting a breach on the red first sweep, on the unrelated manifest fixed in-session, or on
the sweep's breadth would be a false positive.
